              "text": "Hipparchus of Nicaea (c. 190 \u2013 c. 120 BC) was an ancient Greek astronomer, geographer, and mathematician. He is widely regarded as one of the greatest scientific minds of antiquity. While many of his original writings have been lost, his achievements were documented by later scholars like Ptolemy. \n\nHipparchus is credited with inventing, founding, or majorly developing several key tools, systems, and concepts:\n\n### 1. Trigonometry (The Table of Chords)\nHipparchus is universally recognized as the **\"Father of Trigonometry.\"** To solve complex astronomical problems, he developed the first-known trigonometric table, which was a **table of chords** (the straight-line distance between two points on a circle connected by an arc). This was the direct mathematical ancestor of the modern sine and cosine functions. He also popularized dividing a circle into 360 degrees (adapted from Babylonian mathematics) to calculate these angles.\n\n### 2. The Planispheric Astrolabe (Highly Probable)\nAlthough there is some historical debate, many historians credit Hipparchus with inventing the **astrolabe**. An astrolabe is an ancient astronomical \"analog computer\" used to solve problems regarding time, navigation, and the positions of the Sun and stars. He is believed to have applied his understanding of *stereographic projection*\u2014flattening the three-dimensional celestial sphere into a two-dimensional plate\u2014to create the device. \n\n### 3. The Hipparchan Dioptra\nHipparchus invented or significantly improved the **dioptra**, a sighting instrument used in astronomy and surveying. His specific version consisted of a long, grooved rod (about 2 meters long) with a fixed eye-piece at one end and a sliding rectangular plate at the other. By sliding the plate back and forth until it perfectly covered the disk of the Sun or Moon, he was able to calculate their apparent angular diameters with remarkable precision. \n\n### 4. The Stellar Magnitude Scale\nWhen compiling the Western world's first comprehensive star catalog (which indexed around 850 stars), Hipparchus invented a system to classify their brightness. He divided stars into **six categories of magnitude**: the brightest stars were \"first magnitude,\" and the barely visible stars were \"sixth magnitude.\" Astronomers still use a highly precise, modernized version of this magnitude scale.\n\n### 5. An Astronomical Grid for Geography (Latitude and Longitude)\nWhile Eratosthenes had previously proposed a basic coordinate grid for maps, Hipparchus revolutionized geography by inventing a method to determine **exact latitude and longitude using astronomical observations**. He realized that local solar time differs between locations, and argued that comparing the observed times of a lunar eclipse from different cities was the only accurate way to calculate their relative longitudes.\n\n### 6. The Observational Armillary Sphere (Major Improvements)\nWhile simpler versions of the armillary sphere (a physical model of the celestial objects) existed before him, Hipparchus is credited with developing a highly sophisticated, graduated **observational armillary sphere**. He utilized this device\u2014which featured multiple interlocking bronze rings representing celestial coordinates like the equator and the ecliptic\u2014to measure the exact coordinates of stars for his catalog.\n\n---\n\n### His Most Famous Scientific Discovery: Precession of the Equinoxes\nWhile not a physical invention, Hipparchus's most famous conceptual breakthrough was the **discovery of the precession of the equinoxes**. By comparing his own meticulous star observations with records from astronomers who lived 150 years prior, he realized that the positions of the stars were slowly shifting over time. He correctly deduced that the Earth's rotational axis slowly wobbles over a cycle of roughly 26,000 years.",
